'''Llanito''' ({{Literal translation|little plain}}; {{Langx|mxi|لْيَانِيتُو|Lyanítu}}) is a [[Boroughs of Castejón|borough]] of the [[Castejón|Metropolitan City of Castejón]]. It is the third largest borough geographically, and is bordered by the boroughs of [[Charxes]] and [[Coruxón]] to the northeast and northwest.

It was reported to have a population of 3,631,287 in the [[2025 Baliscan census]], making it the most populous borough in the Castejón Metropolis and subsequently the fourth-most populous [[Cantons of Balisca|third-level subdivision]] in the Baliscan Confederation. Were it an independent city, Llanito would be the fifth most-populous in Balisca. Approximately 46.6 percent of the residents of the borough are foreign-born.

The [[W:Urban sprawl|urban sprawl]] of Castejón reached what is now Llanito in the early-20th century, turning farms, former marshes, and lakes into developed areas, but many of the former communes have kept their original layouts, plazas, and narrow streets and have conserved structures built from the 17th to the early 20th centuries. Sixty-six percent of the borough is {{W|Car-free zone|prohibited}} to {{W|Private transport|private}} {{W|motorized traffic}}, and 22% of the borough's land is {{W|Conservation area|protected green space}}. 

Llantio's boroughscape consists of what was once {{w|wetlands}}, with sporadic hills and the waterfront on [[Salto Bay]] bounded by {{W|Restoration ecology|restored}} {{W|urban wild}}, such as the [[Saladares Ecological Zone]]. 

